s  a rude awakening and this movie really  doesn't exist without anya and thomason  what was it about them that made the  sort of perfect counterpart for one  another on screen  well um  the irony is is when  i i talked to anya about it maybe three  years before we'd written the first  draft  and initially i was thinking of her  playing the eloise character but then  when we started writing we started  expanding the sandy character  and by this point i'd seen anya in some  other movies and also i got an idea of  what else she could do so i was thinking  maybe annie should play the 60s part and  she totally agreed and was like i'd love  to play that part so  it was great and then and then we went  looking for eloise which we found in the  amazing form of thomas and mckenzie  you also have this amazing veteran cast  as well you've got  uh you know you've got dame diana rigg  you have margaret nolan you have you  know these just amazing actors was it  easy to get them on board  i mean  yeah in the sense that they all wanted  to do it absolutely like so that was  that in a way like having written a film  that's partly takes place in the 60s  and then having some of these icons be  in the movie and get their perspective  on the script was like you know really  validating for both of us  um  and you know just working with like with  the touching him and tearing stamp just  um  you know it was it was incredible  and what about for you christy is that  sort of a moment when you have these  icons come on board as a film fan is  that just like blowing your mind yeah i  mean i used to watch the avengers like  the diana rig maple avengers when i was  very young she was like the first female  superhero i'd ever watched on screen and  yeah getting to meet her and getting to  hear her you know act words that you had  written is sort of like totally i mean  it's a memorial treasure always it's  incredible that it it is her final role  and it's just she's  not she's so phenomenal in it it was so  great to see her on screen um i like  that one of the themes here is that  nostalgia and looking back at the past  is kind of dangerous it's not you know  what what we  when we're looking at it with  rose-colored glasses are you both people  who  do do you get caught up in nostalgia  when you look back at the past do you  tend to look at it with those  rose-colored glasses i think this film  is my attempt to cure that  so i would say yes so like a hopeless  nostalgic but i think in a way what the  movie is about is like the sort of the  danger of doing that people always talk  about the good old days like there was  some perfect decade where everything was  great and nothing was bad and of course  it's a fallacy it doesn't exist  and same for you yeah the same for me i  just think obviously i would love to be  able to time travel and visit the 60s  but i wouldn't hang around for too long  um and yeah i think nostalgia is so easy  for everyone to be like it would have  been great it would have been amazing  that would have been my decade but live  in the here and now and eloise you know  dreams of being a fashion designer when  you were her age what was the dream for  you was it this  yeah  yeah i mean i  i i sort of was in you know wanted to  kind of be  a director from like when i was i knew i  wanted to do something in film and when  i was about 14 i saw a documentary about  sam raimi making the evil dead at age 18  and i was like  i want to do that  like that's what i want to do and it was  sort of a real like lightning bolt  moment of like going into school the  next day saying okay this is this is  what i'm gonna do with no idea of how to  do it or like no connections with the in  in the industry whatsoever so  then  it was but that was the start of the  journey i guess  i always feel when i'm watching your  movies that you open up this wormhole  where of discovery where people want to  go in and look at you know what movies  what music you're listening to what sort  of is in this world i know sparks  brothers so many people say they've just  discovered sparks  uh with this movie what would you  suggest people watch and listen to after  watching last night in soho  well i mean  there's definitely i don't ever people  to feel that there's required reading  before i think in in a way  like eloise you know you can just go on  the journey with her but um you know i'm  excited for the soundtrack to come out  that's really been asking me about that  and you know that will be out closer to  the release um but you know like i mean  in in when we were writing the movie  like i i had actually sort of just been  diving into kind of like  british cinema the stuff that i hadn't  seen like not so much the psychological  thrillers and horror films like you know  i was so well versed in that so actually  watching a lot of like 60s dramas  the lesser known ones has been that's  just an ongoing gift for me  and i know when we spoke to you for baby  driver you had written the script uh you  know in your cast said like you gave  them a playlist to listen to while they  were reading through the script was that  the same with this one  i think it's the first thing that i said  christy was like here are the songs here  are the songs and the exact songs that  were like now in the finished film and  it was incredible because like usually  music is much later in my other kind of  like working relationships and having  that like i would cycle into our office  listening to the tracks being like okay  today i'm gonna write something that  works with this it's really good fun  and then there's a lot of in this movie  a lot of  mirrors and camera tricks that go into  it can you talk a little bit about some  of the practical stuff that you did  especially with the mirror and down the  stairs sort of scene or the dancing  scene yeah i mean the thing that i hope  uh  you know people kind of like don't  really know exactly how things are done  and you don't really want to reveal  exactly  uh how the tricks work but the thing i'd  say is that more of it is happening for  real in front of you than you would  think  like so there maybe isn't a reliance on  green screen or motion control  in too many sort of scenes there's a lot  of it where it's like just kind of  magic circle stuff like where you know  mirrors are sliding back or we have  double sets or you have  identical twin actors kind of on either  side of the mirror so that stuff was  really  fun to design and then just takes an  enormous amount of rehearsal from all  departments  like you know you have a choreographer  and you and thomason need to be doing  exactly the same thing  the camera operator needs to be in  exactly the right place so they're  tricky shots to pull off but like when  they do work it's such a sort of great  feeling because  you know sometimes when you're making a  movie with effects and people kind of  who are making the movie don't really  know what it's going to look like but  when you pull off a shot in camera  everybody is crowded around the monitor  because it's like hey it works  you know so it's always a thrilling  feeling doing stuff like that and you  said that your mom hasn't seen it yet is  she is she a tough critic  uh i mean i'm really excited for my mum  and dad to see it because i guess my  obsession with the 60s started with them  because  you know they were they were both around  they were like art college in the 60s  and  you know my obsession with the decades  sort of starts with their record  collection  so i'm excited for them to see the movie  and it's been you know it's been so long  since scott pilgrim you've spent so much  time in toronto do you still feel like  an honorary torontonian  i did when i got in this is my first  transatlantic flight in like 20 months  and so when i woke up the next morning  after the flight i went wandering to  find my old apartment  i had a dark horse express though i was  like oh yeah i remember toronto  i know i love it here i you know i would  love the opportunity to come back and  shoot here again if it came out yeah and  i mean hopefully next film back in the  festival so you can experience what it's  like you know in a full festival  experience with the crowds in it well  you know like like i think i said this  last night when we were introducing it  is like we didn't take any of this for  granted i think in this kind of day and  age like people who love cinema and like  going to see something on the big screen  like all of these moments of pressure so  it meant a lot to us to just be there  last night  you

